  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nUncertainty regarding thunderstorm coverage and relatedly heat \nstress concerns continues today with cloud cover and showers \nlikely through this morning complicating the forecast. It seems \nlikely, though, that afternoon highs will creep into the 90s F \nacross the area and coupled with continued seasonably humid \nconditions characterized by dewpoints in the middle 70s F, heat \nindices likely crack 100 F for most and approach 105 F for some. \nFor this reason, a \"Limited\" threat area for dangerous heat \ncontinues to be advertised for mainly our northeast Louisiana \nparishes, southeast Arkansas counties, and portions of Mississippi\nwest of I-55 and in the Pine Belt. \n\nWednesday into next week ...\n\nRain chances continue Wednesday into Friday associated with \ndisturbances through the northwest flow aloft. The likelihood \nthat these disturbances generates showers and a few rumbles of \nthunder versus some risk for strong to marginally severe storms \nwill likely come down to timing. For now, it appears the most \nfavorable window for these disturbances could come in the morning\nhours this morning and again Wednesday morning which would tend \nto limit the degree of destabilization we see each day. For that \nreason, local graphics will not advertise a severe weather threat\nwith this update cycle. Should confidence improve indicating \nsufficient destabilization is likely, a small \"Marginal\" risk area\ncould be introduced for Wednesday. \n\nLooking beyond midweek, the stout upper ridge to our west will \nlikely weaken but begin to translate eastward again along the \nGulf Coast region. Upper heights locally then are likely to only\nmarginally increase but with weaker flow aloft and the ridge more\ncentered near the area, a relatively drier stretch is likely \nheaded into the weekend/early next week. The high will likely \ncontinue it's eastward shift thereafter and rain chances should \ntransition to focus on sea breeze development and air mass \nthunderstorms. /NF/86/",
